1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:08,759
The Cyton is an octagon about 6 centimetres across, with parts on both sides.

2
00:00:08,759 --> 00:00:10,057
Let's start on top.

3
00:00:10,057 --> 00:00:17,707
Along the top edge is the electrode header, a double row of right-angle pins where your electrode wires plug in.

4
00:00:17,707 --> 00:00:23,650
Just below it are small black resistor arrays, part of the protection on every channel.

5
00:00:24,867 --> 00:00:30,234
In the middle sits the most important chip: the ADS1299 from Texas Instruments.

6
00:00:30,234 --> 00:00:36,417
It's an 8-channel amplifier and analog-to-digital converter, designed specifically for EEG.

7
00:00:36,417 --> 00:00:43,347
Around it are lots of small capacitors that keep its power supplies and its internal reference steady.

8
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:51,806
Below it is the PIC32 microcontroller, the board's brain, with its 8 megahertz crystal next to it.

9
00:00:51,806 --> 00:00:59,673
Two small buttons, RST and PROG, let you restart it or load new firmware, and a blue LED shows its status.

10
00:01:00,867 --> 00:01:03,404
Around the edges are black sockets.

11
00:01:03,404 --> 00:01:05,941
J1, on the left, is the Daisy port.

12
00:01:05,941 --> 00:01:09,856
J3 and J4 bring out spare pins of the microcontroller.

13
00:01:09,856 --> 00:01:20,367
Near the bottom is a tiny accelerometer that measures head movement, and next to it an even tinier logic chip, an OR gate, that we'll meet later.

14
00:01:21,533 --> 00:01:23,129
Now flip the board over.

15
00:01:23,129 --> 00:01:30,311
On the underside are the battery plug, the power supply chips, the radio module, and the micro SD card slot.

16
00:01:30,311 --> 00:01:34,965
Right under the electrode pins you'll also find more protection parts:

17
00:01:34,965 --> 00:01:37,093
TVS diodes and capacitor arrays.

18
00:01:38,267 --> 00:01:42,392
On one edge is the slide switch, with three positions:

19
00:01:42,392 --> 00:01:43,614
PC, OFF and BLE.

20
00:01:43,614 --> 00:01:59,655
The schematic, the circuit diagram OpenBCI published, groups all these parts into the same blocks: power supply, input protection, the ADS1299, the microcontroller, the radio, the SD card and the accelerometer.

21
00:01:59,655 --> 00:02:02,787
We'll go through them one flow at a time.

22
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,697
A quick word on how to read a schematic.

23
00:02:06,697 --> 00:02:11,215
Each chip is drawn as a box with its pins numbered around the edge.

24
00:02:11,215 --> 00:02:12,294
Lines are wires.

25
00:02:12,294 --> 00:02:17,082
A dot where lines cross means they're connected; no dot, no connection.

26
00:02:17,082 --> 00:02:28,883
Instead of drawing every long wire, the schematic often uses names, like DVDD or MISO: every point with the same name is connected, even if it's on the other side of the page.

27
00:02:28,883 --> 00:02:35,761
Parts have reference names, like U6 or R5, and the same name is printed next to the part on the board.

28
00:02:35,761 --> 00:02:39,200
That's how you match the drawing to the real thing.

29
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:48,310
Underneath the white surface, the Cyton has four layers of copper: one on top, two inside, and one on the bottom.

30
00:02:48,310 --> 00:02:51,880
The copper traces are the wires from the schematic.

31
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:59,370
Switch the 3D explorer to copper routing, and you can see them: red for the top layer, cyan for the bottom.

32
00:02:59,370 --> 00:03:04,410
Tiny plated holes, called vias, carry signals from one layer to another.

33
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:06,605
Chapter recap.

34
00:03:06,605 --> 00:03:16,366
On top: the electrode header, the protection resistors, the ADS1299, the PIC32 with its crystal, buttons and LED, and the black sockets.

35
00:03:16,366 --> 00:03:23,328
Underneath: the battery plug, the power chips, the radio, the SD card, and more protection parts.

36
00:03:23,328 --> 00:03:27,060
Every part has a name you can find in the schematic.
